-- The handful of definitions the rest of the collection builds on.


-- The simplest `Defines`: a name, and what it is called. `Requires:` states
-- what a use of `\set` must establish; `\\abstract` is the builtin standing for
-- "no further obligation".
[\set]
Defines: X
Requires:
. capability: x_ "in" X :-> \\abstract
Documented:
. called: "set"
Id: "5c194a16-3f2b-4a32-9a03-6fbafb40a8df"


-- A `Defines` target may state a spec relation, which is the type every value
-- of the defined type inherits.
[\element:of{X}]
Defines: x "in" X
when: X is \set
Documented:
. called: "element of $X?$"
Id: "85df16c5-71d3-4e68-b87c-bbb3a85e2df8"


-- A function type describes call behavior: parenthesized specs on both sides,
-- with each parameter written `_`.
[\function:on{A}:to{B}]
Defines: f(x__) is (_ "in" A) -> (_ "in" B)
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "function from $A?$ to $B?$"
Id: "98fa5602-43ab-4c75-b505-1795fa6884c8"


-- An infix command, written `\.name./` between its operands.
[A \.cross./ B]
Declares: X := \set@{(a_, b_) : a_ "in" A; b_ "in" B}
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "cross product of $A?$ and $B?$"
Id: "674ead6f-ab2b-4682-b1a2-1921630d7b52"


-- An operator-form target states its type about the operator itself, so this
-- says `*` is a function on pairs drawn from `X`.
[\operation:on{X}]
Defines: x_ * y_ is \function:on{X \.cross./ X}:to{X}
when: X is \set
Documented:
. called: "operation on $X?$"
Id: "da1c3fe8-35fc-46b0-95ff-63ec39bfd5ef"


-- A value whose type is a function type, used below to realize a successor.
[\identity:on{X}]
Declares: f(x__) is \function:on{X}:to{X}
when: X is \set
Documented:
. called: "the identity on $X?$"
Id: "3c86ff2d-f2e5-4b70-acb6-5c8502af9fe4"
